-- #1111 A-2.13 (#1319 unit 3) — the "FEATURE PRESENT, UNRELATED CODE STILL
-- BEHAVES" cell.  It must be green BEFORE and AFTER the change; that is the
-- property, not a drain.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT IT IS FOR.  Unit 3 makes `resolveFieldRecord`'s arm (a) select a record
-- by the RECEIVER'S IDENTITY out of `universeRecordIdentsRef`, and that path runs
-- on EVERY `.field` access whose receiver head is a known record — not only on the
-- colliding ones.  So the hazard is not "does #1376 drain", it is "does ordinary
-- code that has nothing to do with a collision still resolve".  Every cell below
-- is one of the ways this graph can reach that lookup, and none of them is the
-- shape either issue is about.
--
-- The four cells, and which path each takes:
--
--   `mkQ.shared`  — otherlib's `Other` is UNIQUE graph-wide.  One candidate, and
--                   the identity matches it.  This is the bystander proper: the
--                   assertion is about a record no part of this change is about.
--                   `shared` is deliberately a field name THREE records here
--                   declare, so the field→owners multimap is non-trivial and the
--                   cell also stands over the half unit 2 removed.
--   `lv.shared`   — `Local` is declared by THIS module.  The companion is grown
--                   from `pubDecls` at the END of a module's arm, so a module
--                   never sees its own records in it: the identity lookup MISSES
--                   and the bare-name floor answers, which is today's behaviour.
--                   If that miss path regressed, this cell is what says so.
--   `hxOf mkH`    — the projection happens inside `ahold`, on a NAMED-FIELD
--                   VARIANT (registry key `Plain`, type `Holder`).  Arm (a) never
--                   hits for it — the head is `Holder` and no companion row is
--                   keyed `Holder` — so the field→owners path answers, unchanged.
--   `mkP.px`      — THE DISCRIMINATING CELL: it proves that SOME candidate match
--                   runs at all.  Registry key `Plain` is COLLIDED: `plainlib`
--                   declares a record `Plain` and `ahold` declares a named-field
--                   variant also spelled `Plain` but owning type `Holder`.
--                   Measured by stubbing, not argued: with the candidate match
--                   dropped entirely ("first candidate wins") this cell FAILS —
--                   `Type mismatch: Plain vs Holder` then `Field 'px' does not
--                   belong to record 'Plain'`.
--
-- ⚠️ AND HERE IS WHAT IT DOES *NOT* DISCRIMINATE.  Two drafts of this header have
-- now been wrong about this, in opposite directions, so read the measurement and
-- not the prose.
--   * Dropping only the DECLARING-MODULE conjunct (match on the owning type name
--     alone) leaves this cell GREEN — measured — and reddens
--     `record_receiver_ident_named_vs_held/` instead.
--   * 🚨 Dropping only the OWNING-TYPE-NAME conjunct (match on the declaring
--     module alone) ALSO leaves this cell green — and leaves the ENTIRE TREE
--     green: all 52 `diff_compiler_llvm_modules` cells, `make check-self`, and
--     five purpose-built ≥2-field probes graded on the executed native binary.
--     The previous draft's "so the two conjuncts are covered by two different
--     fixtures" was therefore false: `mkP.px` reds only when BOTH are dropped, so
--     it attributes redness to the PAIR, not to the type-name half.
-- The type-name conjunct is kept as belt-and-braces, with the derivation of why
-- nothing can measure it written at `recordCandIsType` in
-- `compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`.  Do not add a fixture here claiming otherwise.
--
-- ⚠️ THE `hxOf mkH` CELL IS BLIND TO A LAYOUT ERROR, for exactly the reason the
-- two DRAIN cells were: `Holder`'s `hx` and `plainlib`'s `px`/`shared` are
-- DISJOINT, so the field→owners list for `hx` is a SINGLETON and no wrong record
-- is reachable for it to pick.  It grades that the honest-absence route still
-- ANSWERS, not that it answers with the right layout.  Left as-is rather than
-- given a shared field at a differing index, because widening it would change
-- what `mkP.px` discriminates (the cell's load-order note below) and this cell's
-- job is the bystander property, not layout — the layout job belongs to
-- `record_receiver_ident_value_only_field_order/` and its siblings.  Stated here
-- rather than left for a reader to assume, which is the whole lesson of the
-- single-field corpus.
--
-- ⚠️ AND THIS CELL HAS NO OBSERVABLE SUCCESSOR, DELIBERATELY.  Its two sibling
-- round-1 cells (`…_value_only/`, `…_named_vs_held/`) each point at a
-- `…_field_order/` successor because a wrong pick there is SILENT.  This one does
-- not need one: its discriminating projection `mkP.px` fails LOUDLY (`Type
-- mismatch: Plain vs Holder`, then `Field 'px' does not belong to record
-- 'Plain'`) when the match is dropped, so there is no silent face to expose.
-- TWO cells got successors, not three.
--
-- ⚠️ THE CELL DEPENDS ON LOAD ORDER TO FAIL UNDER "FIRST CANDIDATE WINS", AND THAT
-- IS FRAGILE.  `ahold` must be the EARLIER candidate under key `Plain` — the
-- module names are chosen so it is, the same way `field_owner_no_narrowing/`
-- depends on `sortUniqS(["Dup","Wrec"])`.  Renaming these modules can silently
-- weaken the cell against that particular over-widening (it would still catch the
-- type-name half via the `Holder` mismatch only if `ahold` still sorts first).
--
-- Hand-derived correct answer, independent of any engine:
--   20 (mkQ.shared) + 1000 (lv.shared) + 100 (hxOf mkH) + 1 (mkP.px) = 1121.
import ahold.{Holder, mkH, hxOf}
import plainlib.{Plain, mkP}
import otherlib.{Other, mkQ}

data Local = { shared : Int }

lv : Local
lv = Local { shared = 1000 }

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn (intToString (mkQ.shared + lv.shared + hxOf mkH + mkP.px))
